WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.927
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.927 --> 00:00:02.515
Monday the twenty-sixth.

00:00:02.515 --> 00:00:09.199
Full one today, with a shoot after work and a party waiting at the end if you've got the legs for it.

00:00:09.199 --> 00:00:11.582
Something to chew on while you move.

00:00:11.582 --> 00:00:17.472
Raw PCM is just a long stream of numbers, so what's the one thing a WAV file adds on top?

00:00:17.472 --> 00:00:18.796
Answer's at the end.

00:00:18.796 --> 00:00:20.318
Weather's a gift today.

00:00:20.318 --> 00:00:28.657
Sunny, dry, and up into the low seventies by afternoon, which is warm for late October, with only a light breeze for the ride.

00:00:28.657 --> 00:00:32.495
Tomorrow drops back to the low sixties, so enjoy this one.

00:00:32.495 --> 00:00:33.422
Okay, the day.

00:00:33.422 --> 00:00:36.466
Gym at seven, then Godinger from nine to five.

00:00:36.466 --> 00:00:40.437
Somewhere late morning, a lens rental is coming back to you.

00:00:40.437 --> 00:00:42.489
The exact time still isn't set.

00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:50.695
When it shows up, check it against the pickup checklist before it leaves your hands, since the damage cover depends on that.

00:00:50.695 --> 00:00:55.262
After work you've got a portrait shoot at six, wrapping around seven.

00:00:55.262 --> 00:00:58.902
And there's a dumpling party running from five to nine.

00:00:58.902 --> 00:01:03.534
It overlaps the shoot, but you can still catch the last stretch after.

00:01:03.534 --> 00:01:08.299
Grab something small before the shoot so you're not starving through it.

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:09.226
Alright, news.

00:01:09.226 --> 00:01:12.337
Lightroom's phone app can now open closed eyes.

00:01:12.337 --> 00:01:21.073
When it spots someone blinking in a portrait or a group shot, a button pops up, and one tap paints in open eyes with generative A.I.

00:01:21.073 --> 00:01:26.764
You can feed it other photos of the same person so the eyes actually look like theirs.

00:01:26.764 --> 00:01:34.838
Some photographers are annoyed, partly at the feature and partly because the app store listing now calls Lightroom an A.I.

00:01:34.838 --> 00:01:35.699
photo editor.

00:01:35.699 --> 00:01:40.993
For a group shot where one person always blinks, though, you can see the appeal.

00:01:40.993 --> 00:01:43.111
Sticking with gear for a second.

00:01:43.111 --> 00:01:49.266
Sigma started a new line of small, vintage-style lenses that go for character over sharpness.

00:01:49.266 --> 00:01:53.634
The first one's a full-frame fifty millimeter at f one point four.

00:01:53.634 --> 00:02:00.252
It's a funny turn for a company that spent the last decade chasing the sharpest glass it could make.

00:02:00.252 --> 00:02:02.039
And tomorrow, Israel votes.

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:07.267
It's the first election since the Gaza war, and it's a real test for Netanyahu.

00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:11.106
His main challenger is Gadi Eisenkot, a former army chief.

00:02:11.106 --> 00:02:17.989
The final polls point to a stalemate, with neither side reaching the sixty-one seats you need to govern.

00:02:17.989 --> 00:02:23.548
If that holds, it means weeks of coalition bargaining, or possibly another election.

00:02:23.548 --> 00:02:25.401
Here's one for next weekend.

00:02:25.401 --> 00:02:34.600
On Saturday the seventh, Gold Sounds on Wyckoff has a two in the afternoon show, four loud punk and hardcore bands for about fifteen bucks.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.181
Afternoon shows are an underrated move.

00:02:37.181 --> 00:02:43.667
You get the full sweaty set, you're back outside before dark, and the whole evening's still yours.

00:02:43.667 --> 00:02:45.984
Today's challenge is for your head.

00:02:45.984 --> 00:02:50.749
Pick one task at work and give it twenty-five minutes with nothing else.

00:02:50.749 --> 00:02:55.580
No switching tabs, no checking messages, no quick look at something else.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:59.617
If a thought pulls at you, let it wait till the timer's done.

00:02:59.617 --> 00:03:03.654
Then notice how much got done compared to a normal half hour.

00:03:03.654 --> 00:03:07.625
Most people are surprised how rarely they do just one thing.

00:03:07.625 --> 00:03:10.074
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:10.074 --> 00:03:13.648
Remember setting up the memo's feed on a private path?

00:03:13.648 --> 00:03:17.817
Here's how that kind of lock works, and where it stops working.

00:03:17.817 --> 00:03:21.854
An unguessable URL is a link with a long random string in it.

00:03:21.854 --> 00:03:23.178
There's no password.

00:03:23.178 --> 00:03:24.965
The link itself is the key.

00:03:24.965 --> 00:03:28.803
Whoever has it gets in, and whoever doesn't can't find it.

00:03:28.803 --> 00:03:30.590
The guessing part is solid.

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:38.664
A random string of thirty-two characters has more possible values than there are grains of sand on Earth, many times over.

00:03:38.664 --> 00:03:41.973
Nobody's stumbling onto it by trying combinations.

00:03:41.973 --> 00:03:44.290
The weak spot is that links travel.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:48.393
They get pasted in a chat, and the chat app fetches a preview.

00:03:48.393 --> 00:03:51.503
They sit in browser history and in server logs.

00:03:51.503 --> 00:03:55.210
Podcast apps often sync your feeds to their own servers.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:04:00.239
And if the link is ever written somewhere public, it's not a secret anymore.

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:01.695
That's the whole risk.

00:04:01.695 --> 00:04:04.806
Not that someone guesses it, but that it leaks.

00:04:04.806 --> 00:04:09.306
The other limit is that you can't take the key back from one person.

00:04:09.306 --> 00:04:17.050
If a link leaks, the only fix is moving to a new random path, and then everyone who should have it needs the new one.

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:23.072
So it's a good fit for stuff that'd be mildly awkward to have out there, like a daily memo.

00:04:23.072 --> 00:04:27.771
It's not the right lock for anything you'd really mind someone reading.

00:04:27.771 --> 00:04:34.654
So: gym at seven, Godinger till five, check that lens when it comes back, shoot at six, dumplings after.

00:04:34.654 --> 00:04:40.345
The answer: a tiny header saying how fast to play the numbers and how big each one is.

00:04:40.345 --> 00:04:42.000
Have a good Monday, Ziad.
